WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.239
When an error hits, do you retry? And if so, how? That's the question that this lesson answers.

2
00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:10.720
Over the next few minutes, we'll cover how to classify errors as transient or permanent,

3
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.639
how to match the right policy to each error type, how to set a retry budget that keeps

4
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:21.920
cost and latency under control, and when aborting is the right call. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.159
Some errors go away if you wait a second and ask again. Others fail identically,

6
00:00:28.159 --> 00:00:33.360
no matter how many times you ask, and the whole design rests on telling those apart.

7
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.720
A transient error is a temporary failure likely to resolve on its own, like a timeout,

8
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.479
a rate limit hit, or a brief network blip, so retrying is appropriate because the condition

9
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:50.639
may already be gone by the time the next attempt fires. A permanent error won't resolve by retrying

10
00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:57.040
the same request, like incorrect input, permission denied, or a reasoning error in the model's plan.

11
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:03.520
Exponential backoff is the retry strategy where wait time doubles after each failure,

12
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.040
typically with added jitter, which prevents retry storms and gives overloaded systems

13
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.919
room to recover. Every retry decision starts with one question. Is this error likely to resolve

14
00:01:15.919 --> 00:01:22.480
if I try again? That question is the entire foundation of retry design. If yes, the error

15
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:29.680
is transient and some retry policy is appropriate. If no, the error is permanent and retrying only

16
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.599
delays the real fix while spending tokens and adding latency. Getting the classification wrong

17
00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:41.760
in either direction has consequences. Retry a permanent error and you waste resources.

18
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.599
Abort on a transient error and you fail unnecessarily. The classification comes first

19
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:53.839
before you touch policy, delay, or budget. Build that habit early because the exam will test

20
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:59.120
whether you can distinguish those categories reliably under conditions designed to blur the

21
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.839
line. The practical classification question is what category does this error fall into?

22
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.960
Transient errors include rate limit responses, request timeouts, temporary network failures,

23
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.520
and service overload signals. Permanent errors include invalid input format, permission denied,

24
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.080
authentication failures, and reasoning errors in the agent's plan. Some errors are genuinely

25
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.240
ambiguous. A 500 status from an external service could be transient overload or a permanent server

26
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.759
side bug. When you can't tell, use one conservative retry with a delay before treating the error as

27
00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:46.399
permanent. That single retry catches transient cases without spiraling into repeated attempts

28
00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:52.880
against something that isn't going to recover on its own. Two basic retry policies sit below

29
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.559
exponential backoff on the complexity scale. Immediate retry fires the next attempt with no

30
00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:05.679
delay. It's appropriate only for momentary glitches that resolve in milliseconds, which is rare in real

31
00:03:05.679 --> 00:03:12.880
agentic workflows. Spamming retries at full speed under load makes congestion worse, not better. Fixed

32
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.639
delay adds a constant weight between each attempt. It's simpler to implement and much better than

33
00:03:18.639 --> 00:03:25.440
immediate retry in most cases. The limitation is that it doesn't adapt. A fixed delay that works

34
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.440
fine under normal load may be too short during a burst failure event where the downstream system

35
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:38.000
needs more recovery time between attempts. Fixed delay is fine for simple cases, but exponential

36
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.240
backoff handles the full range of real-world failure patterns more reliably. Exponential

37
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.720
backoff with jitter is the standard retry policy for transient errors. Wait time doubles after each

38
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:57.759
failure. One second, two seconds, four seconds, eight, up to a configured cap. The doubling gives the

39
00:03:57.759 --> 00:04:03.600
downstream system progressively more time to recover between each attempt. Jitter adds randomness

40
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.119
to the wait time. Without it, a fleet of clients that all hit the same rate limit will all retry

41
00:04:09.119 --> 00:04:14.320
at the same moment, creating a synchronized wave that immediately re-triggers the limit.

42
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.600
Jitter spreads those retries out and prevents that pattern. The critical constraint is that

43
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:25.440
exponential backoff should only be applied to transient errors. Applying it to permanent errors

44
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.440
just delays failure recognition without any chance of improving the outcome. Matching retry

45
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.440
policy to error type is a practical design discipline. Rate limit errors call for exponential

46
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:44.239
backoff with jitter, and you should respect any retry after header the service sends back. Timeout

47
00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:50.239
errors are usually handled with a short fixed delay or a brief exponential backoff, capped at

48
00:04:50.239 --> 00:04:57.359
one to three retry attempts. Invalid input errors should trigger an immediate abort. Retrying

49
00:04:57.359 --> 00:05:04.399
identical input against the same validation produces identical failure every time. Reasoning errors

50
00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:10.480
in the model's plan are permanent by definition. The model produced an incorrect plan. Retrying

51
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.799
the same prompt doesn't change the plan. Abort and escalate so the plan can be corrected at the source.

52
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:24.320
Retry budgets exist because uncapped retries accumulate cost and latency with no guarantee of

53
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.559
ever succeeding. A retry budget sets the maximum number of attempts before the pipeline gives up

54
00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:37.679
and escalates. Each additional attempt multiplies token spend and adds to total wait time. A pipeline

55
00:05:37.679 --> 00:05:43.040
with no retry budget can spiral into dozens of attempts on a failure that was never going to

56
00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:50.000
resolve. Retry budgets keep pipeline latency bounded and predictable. Design the budget around

57
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.480
acceptable latency and cost limits, not around the probability of eventual success. If the system

58
00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:03.359
hasn't recovered after three attempts with backoff, it likely needs intervention. Abort conditions

59
00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:09.119
are the other side of the retry budget. Two conditions signal that aborting is correct.

60
00:06:09.119 --> 00:06:14.880
First, the retry budget is exhausted. Maximum attempts have been reached, the error is still

61
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:21.359
unresolved, and continuing would only add more cost and latency. Abort and escalate upstream.

62
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:29.359
Second, a permanent error on the first encounter. There's no retry warranted at all. Abort immediately

63
00:06:29.359 --> 00:06:35.279
and surface the error. Aborting early on permanent errors avoids compounding cost on guaranteed

64
00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:42.320
failure. One important clarification. Abort doesn't mean silent failure. Aborting triggers

65
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.799
escalation and surfaces the error to the next component or to a human reviewer. The failure

66
00:06:48.799 --> 00:06:55.600
becomes visible, not hidden. Every retry is a bet that something outside of your code changed between

67
00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:01.519
attempts. That's actually a good lesson for every developer. When you retry the same code, you're

68
00:07:01.519 --> 00:07:06.640
expecting a different result only because something outside of it changed. If the failure

69
00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:12.079
was transient, that is a bet worth making with a backoff and a ceiling on how many retries you

70
00:07:12.079 --> 00:07:17.600
attempt. If it was permanent, you're paying the same bill over and over for the same result.

71
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.920
Classifying the error first is what holds the whole thing together.

72
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.839
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

